# The weight p of the choice inside the loop is a program variable, never assigned.  Where
# p >= 2 the first round reaches the weight outside [0, 1]: such a run is an error from which no
# bound may be proved, worth +oo against an upper bound, so the bound fails there.  With a loop
# the probability of reaching the error is a fixpoint, and the one-direction certificate of an
# upper bound can neither state "it is positive" nor be +oo at an error state.
# Expected: refused.  When an out-of-range weight made the run abort (worth 0) the loop was
# worth 0 at p >= 2 and the bound could be proved there; the loop itself is the geometric one
# of geometric_count_bound_valid.pgcl, whose answer needs a backend this refusal precedes.
nat p;
nat c;
nat x;
c := 0;
x := 1;
while (x = 1) {
	{
		x := 0;
	}[p]{
		c := c + 1;
	}
}
?Ex [c] <= 1
